# Line endings: LF everywhere, no exceptions.
#
# This pipeline runs on two hosts by design -- Windows locally, Linux on Kaggle
# -- and Python's text mode writes os.linesep, so the same script on the same
# data emits LF on one and CRLF on the other. `core.autocrlf` is UNSET in this
# checkout (verified 2026-07-30; an earlier version of this file claimed
# core.autocrlf=true, which was never true), so git records those CR bytes as
# real changes. That is how three result files came to show a 279-line diff with
# zero changed content -- and a diff that large is exactly where a genuinely
# changed number goes unnoticed.
#
# Pinning eol=lf here is the git-layer backstop. The source-layer fix is
# `provenance.write_text_lf()`, which every writer of a text artifact uses.
# Both exist on purpose: the writer keeps new files correct, these rules catch
# anything written by hand or by a tool that bypasses it.
#
# Shell scripts additionally MUST be LF: a `#!/bin/sh` script with CRLF fails on
# Linux/macOS with a misleading "bad interpreter" error, and for a git hook that
# means it silently does not run -- i.e. the Definition-of-Done check disappears
# without announcing itself.

# Default for anything git detects as text.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Result and documentation artifacts: these are the ones a reviewer diffs.
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.bib text eol=lf
.githooks/** text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary -- never touch these bytes. The raw .xlsx is pinned by SHA-256 in
# docs/dataset_card.md; any line-ending conversion would break that hash and,
# with it, every review_id derived from the file's row order (inviolable rule 1:
# data/raw/ is read-only).
*.xlsx binary
*.xls binary
*.pdf binary
*.npy binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pt binary
*.bin binary
